This is a very common confusion when deploying free static sites! It means you are looking at the wrong URL.

When you are inside the GitHub interface ([github.com/TalesPrint/javenyx](https://github.com/TalesPrint/javenyx)), you are looking at the repository—the place where the code lives. GitHub shows you the code snippets (like what you see on the left side of the schematic). It cannot render the website there.

You must view the live, published URL (like what you see on the right side of the schematic) where GitHub Pages actually builds and displays your Javenyx storefront.

Step-by-Step Fix to See the Live Storefront
1. Check the Correct URL:
You need to go to your published Pages URL, not the repository. Your address should look like this:

[https://TalesPrint.github.io/javenyx/](https://TalesPrint.github.io/javenyx/)

(Or, if you have pointed your custom domain as we discussed: [talesprint.com/javenyx](https://talesprint.com/javenyx)).

2. Check Your Settings (If the URL doesn't work):
If you go to that live URL and see a '404 Not Found' error, GitHub Pages might not be active on the repository yet.

Go to Repository Settings > Pages.

Under Build and deployment > Source, ensure Deploy from a branch is selected.

Under Branch, select main and / (root).

Click Save.

3. Check Your File Names:
The schematic highlights a critical check. Inside your repository (viewed on github.com), your main file must be named exactly index.html. If you see index.html.txt or a Jupyter Notebook (.ipynb), the browser cannot load the site. Overwrite those files on GitHub by uploading the correct index.html as described in the Final Implementation Step.

