Okay, let's reset and explain this like you are a complete beginner—someone who knows how to use a web browser and a basic text editor, but nothing about coding or launching a website.

We are going to build a professional-looking online store (called a catalog) to showcase your 504 products.

The Look: It will look and feel like an Amazon or Flipkart Storefront, optimized for mobile phones.

The Navigation: Users will browse large, image-based Category Cards first, and then drill down to see a grid of products in that group.

The Sales Workflow: Instead of immediate online payments, users will add items to a Shopping Cart. When they checkout, the site will automatically generate a single, pre-filled WhatsApp message summarizing their entire order (e.g., "Hi! I want to order: 2 Toothpicks (₹189), 1 Cable Winder (₹149). Total: ₹338"). They just press "Send" to place the order with you on WhatsApp at 7003282856.

The Hosting: Your site will be free, fast, and secure forever, hosted by GitHub Pages. (You only pay your domain registrar for talesprint.com).

Here is the exact, step-by-step master plan to make this happen, starting from absolute zero.

Phase 1: Preparation (Your Local Setup)
Before we start building, we must create a clean, organized workspace on your own computer.

1. Create a Project Folder:

On your computer (e.g., your Desktop or Documents folder), create a new, empty folder named javenyx.

2. Gather Your Branding Images:

You need the two logos from image_14.png (the main JAVENYX logo and the Tales Print parent logo).

Save these images into your local javenyx folder. You must name them exactly like this (all lowercase, use hyphens):

📷 javenyx-logo.png

📷 tales-print-logo.png

3. Set Up Your Tools:

Google Colab: This is what we will use to process your massive spreadsheet data into a file the website can read. It's a free, online Python interpreter. colab.research.google.com

Plain Text Editor: We will write the code for the website using a basic text editor on your computer. Use Notepad (Windows), TextEdit (Mac), or a better free one like Notepad++ or Visual Studio Code. (Do not use Microsoft Word or Apple Pages).

GitHub Account: Sign up for a free account at github.com.

Phase 2: Data Processing (Colab to JSON)
Your raw Excel spreadsheet (tales_print_amazon_seller_data (4).xlsx) contains over 500 products. The website cannot read Excel files. We must use Python to convert it into a JSON file—a structured text file that JavaScript can easily read.

1. Open Google Colab: Go to colab.research.google.com and open a "New Notebook."

2. Upload Your Spreadsheet:

Click the Folder icon 📁 on the left sidebar in Colab to open the Files panel.

Drag and drop your tales_print_amazon_seller_data (4).xlsx file into that panel.

3. Run the Conversion Code:

Create a new code cell, paste the entire block below, and press Shift + Enter to run it.

Note: This code does a lot of important work: it cleans the data, handles the stock status ("Yes"/"No"), normalizes the prices to floats (₹149.0), extracts unique SKUs, and automatically groups your raw Amazon category paths into the focused "sellable" categories you confirmed: Kitchen & Home Utilities, Organization & Storage, Beauty & Travel Essentials, Hardware & Office Supplies, and Gifts & Miscellaneous.

Python
import pandas as pd
import json
import os

# Define the input and output file names
excel_file_name = 'tales_print_amazon_seller_data (4).xlsx'
output_json_name = 'products.json'

# Define your custom 'Sellable' Category mapping (derived from summary sheet)
# This mapping handles all Option B logic.
sellableCategoriesMapping = {
    "Kitchen Tools & Gadgets": "Kitchen & Home Utilities",
    "Home Décor & Lighting": "Organization & Storage",
    "Tools & Hardware": "Hardware & Office Supplies",
    "Bath & Cleaning": "Kitchen & Home Utilities",
    "Home Storage & Organization": "Organization & Storage",
    "Health & Wellness": "Beauty & Travel Essentials",
    "Garden & Outdoor": "Hardware & Office Supplies",
    "Beauty & Personal Care Tools": "Beauty & Travel Essentials",
    "Office & Stationery": "Hardware & Office Supplies",
    "Baby & Kids": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Electronics & Mobile Accessories": "Hardware & Office Supplies",
    "Travel & Bags": "Beauty & Travel Essentials",
    "Car & Motorbike Accessories": "Hardware & Office Supplies",
    "Craft & Party Supplies": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Large Appliances & Fixtures": "Kitchen & Home Utilities",
    "Sports & Fitness": "Beauty & Travel Essentials",
    "Jewellery & Gifting": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Pet Supplies": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Fashion & Footwear Accessories": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Religious & Spiritual": "Gifts & Miscellaneous",
    "Uncategorized": "Gifts & Miscellaneous"
}

# --- Check if the input file exists in the Colab session ---
if not os.path.exists(excel_file_name):
    print(f"Error: Could not find '{excel_file_name}'.")
    print("Please upload the file to the left panel 📁.")
else:
    # 1. Read the spreadsheet tab
    df = pd.read_excel(excel_file_name, sheet_name='Product Listings')

    # --- Step 1: GENERATE grouping columns ---
    amazon_cat_column = 'Category' # The column in your sheet with the full path.

    def parse_amazon_category(cat_str):
        if pd.isna(cat_str):
            return "Uncategorized", "General"
        # Split by ' > ' and clean whitespace
        parts = [p.strip() for p in str(cat_str).split('>')]
        level1 = parts[0] if len(parts) > 0 else "Uncategorized"
        level2 = parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else level1
        return level1, level2

    # Verify the column exists before trying to parse
    if amazon_cat_column in df.columns:
        # Create temporary columns that split the Amazon path
        parsed_cats = df[amazon_cat_column].apply(parse_amazon_category)
        
        # This creates the necessary L1 data column for Option B mapping.
        df['temp_amazon_l1'] = [p[0] for p in parsed_cats]

        # --- Step 2: APPLY your Sellable Category mapping (Option B) ---
        # Map the newly created temporary Amazon L1 to your SELLABLE category
        df['MappedCat'] = df['temp_amazon_l1'].map(sellableCategoriesMapping).fillna("Gifts & Miscellaneous")

        # --- Step 3: Clean and Select necessary columns for eCommerce Checkout ---
        # Keep essential columns for cart and WhatsApp checkout stability.
        df_clean = df[['Title', 'Price (INR)', 'In Stock', 'SKU', 'Product URL', 'Image 1', 'MappedCat']].copy()

        # Rename columns to lightweight keys for JavaScript
        df_clean.columns = ['title', 'price', 'stock', 'sku', 'url', 'image', 'cat']

        # Clean Price: Normalize to float (₹149.0) and fill NaNs
        df_clean['price'] = pd.to_numeric(df_clean['price'], errors='coerce').fillna(0.0)

        # 4. Export basic products.json
        df_clean.to_json(output_json_name, orient='records', indent=2)

        print(f"Success! '{output_json_name}' exported successfully with {len(df_clean)} products!")
        print("Now, go to the left Files panel 📁, click the 3 dots next to products.json, and DOWNLOAD it!")

    else:
        print(f"Error: Could not find the Amazon category column named '{amazon_cat_column}' in '{excel_file_name}'.")
        print(f"Actual columns found: {df.columns.tolist()}")

4. Download the products.json file: After the script finishes and prints "Success!," look at Colab's left Files panel. Click the three dots next to products.json and select Download. Move this file into your local javenyx folder.

